The candidate shape issue isn't slot width alone. For example, with 4 slots of 512 bytes (2048 bytes total) on a 4096 byte atomic unit device, each write corrupts 8 slots (4096/512), exceeding the 4-slot ring. This shows the total ring size relative to atomic unit matters, not slot width alone. T1 fails.

If slot width is fixed at format time (e.g., 4096 bytes) and the device later has a 512 byte atomic unit, writing a root slot requires multiple atomic writes, causing tearing. However, if mkfs reserved insufficient space for larger slot widths (e.g., ring space only for 2048 bytes), mounting on a 4096 atomic unit device fails due to insufficient space. This shows T2's requirement needs proper mkfs space reservation, but T2 itself is correct for safety.

The worst case block reuse delay requirement can be derived from checkpoint interval. Measured checkpoint interval is 200 roots per 200000 operations, so 1000 operations per root. Thus, K must be at least 1000. T3 fails because the requirement exists.

Block reuse delay of 8 operations is dangerous. If checkpoints occur every 1000 operations, blocks from root 1 might be reused by root 9. A crash between root 8 and root 9 could cause recovery using root 8 to reference reused blocks, corrupting data.

Reject T. Narrowed version: slot width must match atomic unit at mount time, ring size must be at least atomic unit size, and K must equal checkpoint interval (e.g., 1000 operations).
